import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    private int[][] memo;

    public int change(int amount, int[] coins) {
        Arrays.sort(coins);
        
        memo = new int[coins.length][amount + 1];
        for (int[] row : memo) {
            Arrays.fill(row, -1);
        }
        
        return dfs(0, 0, coins, amount);
    }

    private int dfs(int index, int currentAmount, int[] coins, int amount) {
        // Base case: formed exact target amount
        if (currentAmount == amount) {
            return 1;
        }

        // Return memoized result
        if (memo[index][currentAmount] != -1) {
            return memo[index][currentAmount];
        }

        int totalWays = 0;

        // Try using coins starting from current index to avoid duplicate combinations
        for (int i = index; i < coins.length; i++) {
            if (currentAmount + coins[i] > amount) {
                break; // Array is sorted, so larger coins will also exceed
            }
            
            totalWays += dfs(i, currentAmount + coins[i], coins, amount);
        }

        return memo[index][currentAmount] = totalWays;
    }
}


//CODE END
TIME AND SPACE COMPLEXITY

Time Complexity: O(N * amount)
- Explanation: There are N distinct coin types and target amounts from 0 to 'amount', creating N * (amount + 1) total unique subproblems (states). Since each subproblem is computed once and stored in our memoization array dp[i][j], every subsequent lookup runs in O(1) time.

Space Complexity: O(N * amount)
- Explanation:
  1. Memoization Array: The 2D table Integer[][] dp requires O(N * amount) memory to store subproblem outcomes.
  2. Recursion Stack Depth: In the worst-case call chain, the call stack goes up to O(N + amount) deep.
  3. Total Auxiliary Space: O(N * amount) + O(N + amount) = O(N * amount).
